### 2053 — libssh2 socket teardown has capacity independent of ordinary operation pressure (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- ROOT CAUSE: Socket close used the same 64-slot timeout-race registry as ordinary injected and channel work. Teardown cancelled that registry and immediately tried to register close; while cancellation-ignoring children still occupied all slots, admission failed and `try?` skipped `SSHTCPConnection.close()` before dropping the connection reference.
- REWORK: Teardown now owns a separate fixed two-slot registry: one slot for the socket-close deadline and one for the automatic-task join deadline. Reserved admission is non-throwing under the single teardown owner, so ordinary saturation cannot bypass exact-once socket close; unfinished teardown children remain visible in the owned-resource snapshot until they retire.
- CONFORMANCE: A deterministic regression fills the ordinary registry to 64 while handshake readiness is suspended, starts close before releasing any operation, proves bounded public return and exactly one socket close, then releases late readiness and verifies no bridge read/write plus eventual zero ownership.
- VERIFICATION: `swift test --filter LibSSH2` passes 40 tests/3 suites; `make validate-core` passes 374 tests/31 suites plus boundary checks and build; pinned libssh2/rekey and source-tamper gates, the dual-architecture extension-safe macOS provider/harness matrix, strict Swift format, and diff checks all exit 0. `task-board validate` exits 0 with the existing story aggregate mismatch (`to-dev` versus child `development`) still recorded rather than bypassed.

### 2032 — libssh2 teardown owns late sockets and bounds lifecycle reconciliation (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- ROOT CAUSE: Teardown established `transportClose` only after awaiting the injected socket close, automatic tasks, and every bridge service. Cooperative timeout-race children could still be visible when teardown returned, while a connector success arriving after its timeout was discarded without closing the returned socket.
- REWORK: The close deadline now starts at teardown entry. Socket close, automatic-task joins, bridge drain, and owned-operation reconciliation are bounded; cooperative children receive a short drain grace while genuinely non-cooperative work remains retained and counted. Timeout races accept an async abandoned-result disposer, and TCP connect uses it to close every late socket exactly once.
- SAFETY: The callback bridge checks terminal state after each suspended readiness/read/write boundary. A late socket completion can retire its tracked service but cannot mutate discarded buffers or report post-shutdown network progress.
- CONFORMANCE: New deterministic fixtures cover a cancellation-ignoring connector and socket teardown, prove prompt public return, late-socket close, no post-shutdown write, and eventual zero ownership. The automatic keepalive fixture now waits for its next manual-clock sleeper before advancing.
- VERIFICATION: `swift test --filter LibSSH2` passes 39 tests/3 suites; `make validate-core` passes 373 tests/31 suites plus boundary checks and build; pinned packaging, source-tamper gates, the dual-architecture extension-safe macOS provider/harness matrix, strict Swift format, and diff checks all exit 0.

### 2011 — libssh2 adapter bounds cancellation-ignoring injected work (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
- ROOT CAUSE: The generic timeout helper selected the deadline result but then drained its structured task group, so a resolver, connector, host policy, credential provider, or upload source that ignored task cancellation could hold the public operation and transport close beyond their configured deadlines.
- REWORK: Timeout races now detach callers promptly while a bounded registry retains, cancels, and reports unfinished injected work until it actually returns. Upload keeps the libssh2 channel state machine actor-owned and applies one absolute deadline across open, source read, bounded drains, writes, EOF, and exit; only the injected source read is detached. External signer work follows the same visible ownership rule instead of making teardown wait indefinitely.
- CONFORMANCE: Deterministic cancellation-ignoring resolver, upload-source, and signer fixtures prove operation/close return before release, retained work remains visible in the owned-resource snapshot, and explicit release restores tasks, channels, sockets, sessions, custom allocations, and buffers to baseline.
